# Full studies: faith, works, justification, assurance. Loaded by tools/vapol.py (exec). Scripture: Douay-Rheims unless noted.
# Fields: ctx, who, lang, steps[[h,t]], fathers[[who,text]], obj[[objection,reply]], talk, avoid, read[]

D('luke',23,43,
 ctx="Luke alone records the conversation between Jesus and the two criminals crucified with him (23:39–43). One mocks him: “If thou be Christ, save thyself and us.” The other rebukes him: “Neither dost thou fear God, seeing thou art under the same condemnation? And we indeed justly: for we receive the due reward of our deeds. But this man hath done no evil.” Then he prays: “Lord, remember me when thou shalt come into thy kingdom.” Jesus answers: “Amen I say to thee, this day thou shalt be with me in paradise.”\n\nThe scene sits inside Luke’s great theme of mercy to sinners (the lost sheep, the prodigal son, Zacchaeus) and comes at the moment when Christ is offering his sacrifice. The thief’s salvation is the first fruit of the Cross.",
 who="The argument is used in two ways. (1) Against baptism: the thief was never baptized, yet he was saved, so baptism cannot be necessary for salvation; it is an outward sign of an inward faith that already saves. (2) Against works and the sacraments generally: he had no time to do good works, receive Communion, go to confession or join a church, yet Jesus promised him paradise, so salvation is by faith alone, received in a single moment of trust.\n\nIt is also used (3) against purgatory: he went to paradise “this day”, with no purification.\n\nThis reading is common in Baptist, evangelical and non-denominational preaching, and in the ‘sinner’s prayer’ tradition, where the thief is the model convert.",
 lang="“This day” (sēmeron) is emphatic in Greek and belongs with the promise (“today you will be with me”). A few Protestant groups that deny the soul’s survival after death (Seventh-day Adventists, Jehovah’s Witnesses) move the comma: “I say to you today, you will be with me”. Catholics and most Protestants rightly reject that, since Luke uses “today” for the arrival of salvation (2:11; 4:21; 19:9).\n\n“Paradise” (paradeisos, a Persian loan-word for a walled garden) was used in Jewish writing of the time for the abode of the righteous dead, sometimes called “Abraham’s bosom” (Luke 16:22). Paul uses it for the third heaven (2 Corinthians 12:4).",
 steps=[
  ["The thief lived and died under the Old Covenant","Christian baptism was commanded by the risen Christ (Matthew 28:19) and first administered at Pentecost (Acts 2:38–41). When the thief died, the command had not yet been given, and the New Covenant was being sealed at that very hour. You cannot argue from someone who died before a law was promulgated to the conclusion that the law does not bind those who come after. The thief was no more bound to Christian baptism than Abraham was."],
  ["Catholic teaching already includes the thief","The Church does not teach that God is bound by the sacraments, only that we are. “God has bound salvation to the sacrament of Baptism, but he himself is not bound by his sacraments” (CCC 1257). Those who die before they can be baptized but with a sincere desire, repentance and charity are saved by the ‘baptism of desire’, and martyrs by the ‘baptism of blood’ (CCC 1258–1260; Trent, Session 6, ch. 4: justification comes through baptism “or the desire thereof”). The thief is a textbook case of both: he desires Christ’s kingdom and dies confessing him. So the passage confirms Catholic teaching rather than contradicting it."],
  ["He was not saved by a bare act of mental trust","Look at what the thief actually does in his last hour. He fears God. He confesses his sins openly and accepts his punishment as just (repentance and satisfaction). He defends Christ publicly when everyone else mocks (confession of faith before men, Matthew 10:32). He rebukes his companion (fraternal correction, an act of charity). He prays to Christ as King. That is not ‘faith alone’ in the Reformation sense; it is faith working through charity (Galatians 5:6), which is exactly what Trent says justifies. He did every good work it was possible for a crucified man to do."],
  ["An exception proves the rule; it does not abolish it","Every other conversion in the New Testament after Pentecost includes baptism: the 3,000 at Pentecost (Acts 2:41), the Samaritans (8:12), the Ethiopian (8:38), Saul (9:18; 22:16 “be baptized, and wash away thy sins”), Cornelius, even after receiving the Spirit (10:47–48), Lydia (16:15), the jailer at Philippi (16:33). The apostles never once cite the thief as a reason to skip baptism. A man pulled from a shipwreck without a lifeboat does not prove that ships may sail without lifeboats."],
  ["We do not know he was unbaptized","Scripture is silent. Some early writers speculated that he might have received John’s baptism earlier, or that he was baptized by the water from Christ’s side. Augustine at first used him as an example of baptism supplied by faith and conversion, and later noted that we cannot be certain he was not baptized. The honest point is that the argument from silence cannot carry the weight the objection puts on it. But the Catholic answer does not depend on this; see step 2."],
  ["“Paradise” does not refute purgatory","On Good Friday, Christ’s soul descended to the dead (1 Peter 3:19; CCC 632–635), to the righteous who awaited redemption. Heaven in the full sense, the vision of God, was opened by the Ascension (John 20:17: “I am not yet ascended to my Father”). So ‘paradise’ that day was the company of Christ among the just. Even if the thief entered glory at once, purgatory is not a necessary stage for everyone: martyrs and perfectly contrite souls need no further purification, and the thief’s suffering, accepted in union with Christ, is the most powerful penance imaginable (ST III q.52 a.4 ad 3; Suppl.)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Cyprian of Carthage, Letter 72 (73), 22 (c. 256)","Catechumens who are martyred before baptism are not deprived of the sacrament, since they are baptized with the most glorious baptism of blood (paraphrased). The principle the Church applies to the thief."],
  ["Augustine, On Baptism, against the Donatists IV.22.29 (c. 400)","Augustine uses the thief to show that where baptism cannot be had, faith and conversion of heart can supply what is wanting, provided the sacrament is not despised (paraphrased)."],
  ["Augustine, Retractations II.18 (c. 427)","Reviewing his own works, he notes that the thief is not a certain example, since we do not know whether he had been baptized (paraphrased). A model of intellectual honesty."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST III q.66 a.11; q.68 a.2","Three ‘baptisms’: of water, of blood, of desire (‘of the Spirit’). A man may be saved without the sacrament actually received, but not if he despises it; the desire of baptism comes from faith working by love."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 6, ch. 4 (1547)","The passage from sin to grace cannot happen “without the laver of regeneration, or the desire thereof”."]],
 obj=[
  ["“If baptism of desire saves, then baptism is optional.”","No. Desire of baptism saves only someone who cannot receive it. A person who knows Christ commanded baptism and refuses it does not have the desire; he has the opposite. That is why Trent says ‘or the desire thereof’, and why Thomas adds: provided the sacrament is not despised (III q.68 a.2)."],
  ["“You keep adding conditions. The thief just believed.”","The text records what he believed and did: fear of God, confession of guilt, defence of Christ, prayer. Which of those would an evangelical say is unnecessary? None. Everyone agrees saving faith repents and confesses Christ. The real question is whether that faith then obeys Christ’s commands when it is able, and every convert in Acts shows it does."],
  ["“Catholics say you need the Church, but the thief had no church.”","He was in the presence of the Head of the Church at the moment the Church was born from his side (John 19:34; CCC 766). Every saved person is united to Christ, and therefore to his Body, by grace, even when not by visible membership (CCC 846–848)."],
  ["“Then why do you insist on baptism for babies who cannot believe?”","Because baptism is God’s act, not our achievement. Infants receive the gift as Israelite boys received circumcision, the sign of the covenant, on the eighth day (Colossians 2:11–12). ‘The promise is to you and to your children’ (Acts 2:39). Whole households were baptized (Acts 16:15, 33; 1 Corinthians 1:16)."]],
 talk="“The thief is one of my favourite passages too. But notice three things. First, he died before Jesus commanded baptism, so he couldn’t disobey a command that didn’t exist yet. Second, Catholics have always taught that someone who wants baptism but can’t get it, and dies repenting, can be saved; the thief is the classic example. Third, look at what he actually did: he feared God, admitted his guilt, defended Jesus in public and prayed. That’s faith that loves and acts, and that’s what Catholics mean by saving faith. So the thief doesn’t teach us to skip what Jesus commanded. He teaches us never to give up on anyone, right up to the last breath.”",
 avoid="Do not insist that the thief must have been baptized: Scripture does not say so, and Augustine himself withdrew that claim as uncertain. Do not say ‘paradise means purgatory’: it does not. And do not belittle deathbed conversions; the Church rejoices in them (Matthew 20:1–16).",
 read=["CCC 1257–1261 (necessity of baptism), 632–637 (descent to the dead)","ST III q.66 a.11; q.68 a.2","Council of Trent, Session 6, ch. 4","Jimmy Akin (2010) The Salvation Controversy. El Cajon: Catholic Answers, ch. on baptism","Augustine, On Baptism IV.22.29; Retractations II.18"])

D('ephesians',2,8,
 ctx="Ephesians 2:1–10 describes conversion in three stages. Before: “you were dead in your offences and sins” (2:1), “by nature children of wrath” (2:3). God’s act: “God, who is rich in mercy, for his exceeding charity wherewith he loved us, even when we were dead in sins, hath quickened us together in Christ (by whose grace you are saved)” (2:4–5). After: “For by grace you are saved through faith: and that not of yourselves, for it is the gift of God. Not of works, that no man may glory. For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus in good works, which God hath prepared that we should walk in them” (2:8–10).\n\nThe rest of the chapter (2:11–22) is about Jews and Gentiles being made one body, with the wall of the Law broken down (2:14–15). So ‘works’ here carries the shadow of the Law that divided Jew from Gentile.",
 who="Protestant preaching often treats Ephesians 2:8–9 as the clearest statement of the gospel: salvation is a free gift received by faith; anything we do contributes nothing, otherwise we could ‘boast’. Since Catholics speak of merit, sacraments, penance and good works as necessary for salvation, they are said to teach salvation by works and to deny grace.\n\nThe strongest form: ‘Paul excludes works from salvation without qualification (“not of works”), and gives the reason: so that no one may boast. Any system in which human cooperation makes the difference between the saved and the lost gives grounds for boasting. Catholicism is such a system.’",
 lang="“You are saved” (este sesōsmenoi) is a perfect passive periphrastic: you have been saved and remain in that state. Paul speaks of salvation in three tenses: we were saved (Romans 8:24, aorist), are being saved (1 Corinthians 1:18, present), and shall be saved (Romans 5:9–10, future). No single verse captures the whole.\n\n“And that not of yourselves” (kai touto ouk ex hymōn): ‘that’ (touto) is neuter, while ‘grace’ and ‘faith’ are feminine, so it most naturally refers to the whole event of being saved by grace through faith. Even faith is a gift, which Catholics fully affirm (Council of Orange, 529, canon 5; Trent, Session 6, ch. 8).\n\n“Workmanship” (poiēma, whence ‘poem’) and “created… in good works” (ktisthentes… epi ergois agathois): good works are the purpose of the new creation, ‘prepared beforehand’ (proētoimasen) by God.",
 steps=[
  ["Catholics affirm every word of 2:8–9","Trent, the council that answered the Reformers, says: we are “said to be justified freely, because none of those things which precede justification, whether faith or works, merit the grace itself of justification” (Session 6, ch. 8). The Council of Orange (529) taught that even the beginning of faith is a gift of grace (canon 5). The Catechism: “The merits of our good works are gifts of the divine goodness” (CCC 2009). No Catholic believes we can earn the first grace or initial justification."],
  ["Read verse 10","Paul does not stop at verse 9. We are “created in Christ Jesus in good works”. Works are not the root of salvation but its fruit, and they are not optional decorations; they are what the new creation is for. Catholic teaching is the whole paragraph: grace (v.8), not earned (v.9), producing works God himself prepared (v.10)."],
  ["The works Paul excludes are works done to earn grace","‘Not of works, that no man may glory’ excludes works considered as our own achievement, done apart from grace, giving a claim on God. Works done in Christ by grace are “his workmanship”; boasting in them is boasting in God (1 Corinthians 1:31; 15:10: “not I, but the grace of God with me”). So Catholic merit, which is grace crowning grace, gives no ground for boasting. Augustine: when God crowns our merits, he crowns nothing but his own gifts (Letter 194, 5.19)."],
  ["The verse does not say ‘faith alone’","Paul says “through faith”. Catholics agree that faith is the beginning, foundation and root of justification (Trent, Session 6, ch. 8). But Paul never adds ‘alone’; the only verse with that phrase denies it (James 2:24). And Paul’s ‘faith’ is the faith that ‘worketh by charity’ (Galatians 5:6), without which ‘I am nothing’ (1 Corinthians 13:2)."],
  ["The same Paul makes works necessary for final salvation","God “will render to every man according to his works: to them indeed who, according to patience in good work, seek glory and honour and incorruption, eternal life” (Romans 2:6–7). “If you live according to the flesh, you shall die” (Romans 8:13). “Work out your salvation with fear and trembling” (Philippians 2:12). Paul is not contradicting himself; he is distinguishing the gift that starts the Christian life from the cooperation that the gift makes possible."],
  ["Who is the real target?","In Ephesians 2:11–22 Paul turns to the ‘works’ that divided Jew and Gentile: circumcision and the ‘law of commandments contained in decrees’ (2:15). Jewish Christians could be tempted to ‘boast’ in their covenant status. The verse is first about that: no ethnic or ritual privilege saves; grace does, for Jew and Gentile alike."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Council of Orange (529), canons 5–7","Both the increase and the beginning of faith, and the very desire to believe, are from grace, not from nature. A Catholic council, a thousand years before Luther."],
  ["Augustine, Letter 194, 5.19 (c. 418)","When God crowns our merits, he crowns nothing other than his own gifts."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Ephesians, ch. 2, lect. 3","On ‘not of works’: works are excluded as a cause meriting grace, but grace is given so that we may do good works; works follow grace, they do not precede it (paraphrased)."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 6, chs. 8 and 16 (1547)","Justified ‘freely’ because nothing before justification merits it; eternal life is both grace mercifully promised and a reward faithfully given for good works done in grace."],
  ["Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification (1999)","Lutherans and Catholics confess together: “By grace alone, in faith in Christ’s saving work and not because of any merit on our part, we are accepted by God” (§15). Disagreements remain, but not about Ephesians 2:8–9."]],
 obj=[
  ["“If works are necessary in any sense, salvation isn’t a gift.”","An inheritance is a gift, yet an heir can forfeit it by renouncing the family. A gift that transforms the receiver and is meant to be used is still a gift. Paul calls eternal life both ‘the grace of God’ (Romans 6:23) and the reward of ‘patience in good work’ (Romans 2:7). If Paul can hold both, so can we."],
  ["“Merit means boasting.”","Not if the merit is itself grace. ‘What hast thou that thou hast not received? And if thou hast received, why dost thou glory?’ (1 Corinthians 4:7). Catholic merit is God rewarding what he himself works in us (CCC 2006–2011). Scripture uses the language of reward constantly (Matthew 5:12; 16:27; 25:34–40; 2 Timothy 4:8)."],
  ["“Catholics have to do things to stay saved, so they are earning it.”","A married couple must remain faithful to stay married; nobody says they ‘earn’ their marriage. Remaining in grace means not rejecting it by grave sin (1 Corinthians 6:9–10; Galatians 5:19–21). Even perseverance is a gift (Trent, Session 6, ch. 13)."]],
 talk="“I agree with Ephesians 2:8–9 completely, and so does the Catholic Church: the Council of Trent says nothing before justification, not even faith, earns grace. But keep reading: verse 10 says we are created in Christ Jesus for good works that God prepared for us. Grace isn’t only a pardon; it’s a new creation that produces good works. We don’t work to get saved. We work because we have been saved, and God, who gives the grace, also rewards what his grace does in us.”",
 avoid="Do not say ‘we are saved by faith and works’ as if they were two independent contributions; that is exactly the caricature Protestants fear. Say: by grace, through faith, working through love. And do not deny that Catholics in practice sometimes act as if they could earn heaven; admit it, and point to what the Church actually teaches.",
 read=["Council of Trent, Session 6 (1547), Decree on Justification, chs. 5–8, 16","CCC 1987–2029","Council of Orange (529), canons 1–8","Lutheran World Federation and Catholic Church (1999) Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Ephesians, ch. 2"])

D('romans',3,28,
 ctx="Romans 1–3 argues that both Gentiles (1:18–32) and Jews (2:1–3:20) stand guilty before God, so that “by the works of the law no flesh shall be justified before him” (3:20). Then Paul announces that “the justice of God without the law is made manifest… by faith of Jesus Christ, unto all and upon all them that believe in him” (3:21–22). Verse 28 sums up: “For we account a man to be justified by faith, without the works of the law.” The very next verses explain why it matters: “Is he the God of the Jews only? Is he not also of the Gentiles? Yes, of the Gentiles also. For it is one God, that justifieth circumcision by faith, and uncircumcision through faith” (3:29–30). Chapter 4 then uses Abraham, justified before he was circumcised (4:9–12).",
 who="This is the verse at the heart of the Reformation. Luther translated it in 1522 as “allein durch den Glauben” (‘by faith alone’), adding the word ‘alone’, and defended the addition in his Open Letter on Translating (1530): German idiom needs ‘alone’ to bring out Paul’s sense. The Reformation formula sola fide follows: justification is received by faith alone, apart from any works, including works of charity done in grace.\n\nThe strongest form: ‘Paul excludes “works of the law”, and “law” in Romans includes the moral law (3:20: “by the law is the knowledge of sin”; 7:7 “thou shalt not covet”). If even keeping the moral commandments does not justify, then nothing but faith does, and adding “alone” only makes explicit what Paul means.’",
 lang="Greek: logizometha gar dikaiousthai pistei anthrōpon chōris ergōn nomou. ‘For we reckon a man to be justified by faith apart from works of law.’ There is no word for ‘alone’ (monos) in any Greek manuscript.\n\n“Works of the law” (erga nomou) is a technical phrase. Among the Dead Sea Scrolls, a text known as 4QMMT (c. 150 BC) lists ‘some of the works of the law’, mostly purity and calendar rules that marked off the faithful community. Paul’s argument in 3:29–30 and chapter 4 (circumcision) shows he especially means the Torah as the covenant badge of Israel.\n\nThe only place in the New Testament where pistis and monon (‘only’) occur together is James 2:24: “Do you see that by works a man is justified, and not by faith only (ouk ek pisteōs monon)?”",
 steps=[
  ["The word ‘alone’ is not in the text","Every Greek manuscript lacks it. Luther knew this and defended it as a translation choice, writing in 1530 that the papists stare at the four letters s-o-l-a like cows at a new gate (paraphrased from his Sendbrief vom Dolmetschen). The question is whether the addition is faithful to Paul’s meaning. The only text that pairs ‘faith’ and ‘only’ says the opposite (James 2:24)."],
  ["Paul’s own explanation is about Jew and Gentile","Verse 29 begins with ‘Or’ (ē): ‘Or is he the God of the Jews only?’ The point of 3:28 is that justification does not come through becoming a Jew under the Mosaic covenant, so it is open to Gentiles on equal terms. Chapter 4 proves it from Abraham: righteousness was reckoned to him ‘not in circumcision, but in uncircumcision’ (4:10). This is the ‘works of the law’ Paul has in view: circumcision, Sabbath, food laws, the whole Torah as a covenant charter."],
  ["Even if the moral law is included, ‘works of the law’ are works done without grace","Catholics accept that keeping the moral law by our own strength cannot justify; Paul says the Law gave knowledge of sin but not power over it (7:7–25). But Paul never says works done in Christ, by the Spirit, are irrelevant. In the same letter: “Not the hearers of the law are just before God: but the doers of the law shall be justified” (2:13), and “if by the Spirit you mortify the deeds of the flesh, you shall live” (8:13). The Spirit fulfils the law in us (8:4); love is the fulfilling of the law (13:10)."],
  ["Paul and James answer different errors","Paul opposes the idea that the Torah (and human effort without grace) makes us right with God. James opposes a ‘faith’ that is mere assent and produces nothing: “the devils also believe and tremble” (James 2:19). Both use Abraham. Paul looks at Genesis 15:6 (faith before circumcision); James at Genesis 22 (faith perfected in obedience). Catholic teaching holds both: faith is the start and root, and living faith works through love (Galatians 5:6)."],
  ["Faith in Paul is never mere trust without obedience","Paul’s mission is to bring about “the obedience of faith” (hypakoē pisteōs) among the nations (Romans 1:5; 16:26). In Romans 10:16 ‘they did not all obey the gospel’ is parallel to ‘who hath believed our report?’ Faith in Paul includes surrender of the whole person. The Catholic definition is the same: faith is the submission of intellect and will to God revealing (Dei Verbum 5; CCC 143)."],
  ["What Trent condemned","Trent did not condemn the phrase ‘justified by faith’. It condemned the claim that the sinner is justified by faith alone, in the sense that nothing else is required to cooperate in obtaining justification (canon 9), or that justifying faith is nothing but confidence that one’s sins are forgiven (canon 12). It affirms that faith is ‘the beginning of human salvation, the foundation and root of all justification’ (ch. 8)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, On Faith and Works 14.21 (c. 413)","Augustine says that even in the apostles’ day some misunderstood Paul as teaching that faith without works suffices, and that this is why James, Peter, John and Jude wrote so strongly against it (paraphrased)."],
  ["Augustine, On the Spirit and the Letter 26.45 (412)","Commenting on 2:13: the doers of the law shall be justified, not as if they were doers first and justified afterwards, but justified so that they might become doers (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Romans, ch. 3, lect. 4","The ‘works of the law’ excluded are both ceremonial and moral works considered as done by human effort before grace; works done after justification flow from it. Thomas can say ‘by faith alone’ elsewhere (Commentary on 1 Timothy, ch. 1, lect. 3), meaning faith as opposed to the ceremonies of the Law, never faith without charity."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 6, canons 9, 12, 24 (1547)","Condemns ‘faith alone’ as excluding all cooperation; affirms that justification, once received, is increased by good works."],
  ["Luther, Preface to James (1522)","Called James ‘an epistle of straw’ compared with Paul’s letters, because it ‘has nothing of the nature of the gospel about it’. Showing that the tension with James was felt from the start."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Some Church Fathers used ‘faith alone’ too.”","True. Origen, Ambrosiaster, Chrysostom and Thomas occasionally use ‘by faith alone’ (sola fide). But they mean faith without the works of the Mosaic Law, or the faith by which a convert first comes to God, not a faith that excludes charity and obedience. The words are the same; the doctrine is different."],
  ["“James means ‘shown to be righteous before men’, not justified before God.”","James’ question is ‘can faith save him?’ (2:14), which is salvation before God. And Abraham was ‘justified by works, offering up Isaac’ (2:21), an act no human saw except Isaac. James’ verdict, ‘faith without works is dead’ (2:26), is about whether faith is alive, not how it looks."],
  ["“Works of the law includes the moral law, so all works are excluded.”","Grant it for the sake of argument. Paul still excludes works as our own achievement before grace, not the Spirit’s fruit in the justified. He says ‘faith that worketh by charity’ is what avails (Galatians 5:6), and that without charity even faith that moves mountains is ‘nothing’ (1 Corinthians 13:2). A faith that is nothing cannot justify."]],
 talk="“The word ‘alone’ isn’t in Romans 3:28; Luther added it in 1522 and admitted he added it. The only Bible verse that puts ‘faith’ and ‘only’ together is James 2:24, and it says ‘not by faith only’. Look at Paul’s next sentence: ‘Is God the God of the Jews only?’ His point is that you don’t have to become a Jew and keep the Law of Moses to be saved. Catholics agree completely. We’re justified by faith, a living faith that works through love, as Paul says in Galatians 5:6.”",
 avoid="Do not say Paul and James contradict each other or that Paul is ‘balanced out’ by James; both are inspired and agree. Do not claim Catholics reject ‘justification by faith’; we reject faith alone in the sense Trent defined. And do not dismiss the Reformers’ pastoral concern; much late medieval preaching did obscure grace.",
 read=["Council of Trent, Session 6, chs. 7–10, canons 9–12, 24","CCC 1987–1995, 1814–1816","Augustine, On Faith and Works; On the Spirit and the Letter","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Romans, ch. 3, lect. 4","Luther (1530) Sendbrief vom Dolmetschen [Open Letter on Translating]","N.T. Wright (2009) Justification. London: SPCK (a Protestant scholar on ‘works of the law’)"])

D('galatians',2,16,
 ctx="Galatians is Paul’s fiercest letter. Some Jewish Christians (‘the circumcision’) were telling Gentile converts in Galatia that they had to be circumcised and keep the Mosaic Law to be full members of God’s people. In 2:11–14 Paul recounts confronting Peter at Antioch, who had stopped eating with Gentile Christians for fear of these men. Then: “knowing that man is not justified by the works of the law, but by the faith of Jesus Christ; we also believe in Christ Jesus, that we may be justified by the faith of Christ, and not by the works of the law: because by the works of the law no flesh shall be justified” (2:16). The letter’s practical conclusion: “in Christ Jesus neither circumcision availeth any thing, nor uncircumcision: but faith that worketh by charity” (5:6).",
 who="Galatians is read as Paul’s manifesto against all religion of works: any attempt to add something to faith in Christ (circumcision then; sacraments, penance, merit, saints, Mass now) is ‘another gospel’ (1:6–9). Catholicism, it is argued, is the Galatian heresy reborn: Christ plus human additions.",
 lang="“Works of the law” (erga nomou) appears three times in this verse. ‘Faith of Jesus Christ’ (pistis Iēsou Christou) can be read as faith in Christ or as Christ’s own faithfulness; scholars are divided, and both readings place the saving initiative with Christ.\n\nIn 5:6 the decisive phrase is pistis di’ agapēs energoumenē: ‘faith working (energized) through love’. Energoumenē is the root of ‘energy’: love is the activity of living faith.",
 steps=[
  ["The issue in Galatia was circumcision and the Torah","The whole letter turns on it: Titus was not compelled to be circumcised (2:3); ‘if you be circumcised, Christ shall profit you nothing’ (5:2); the false teachers ‘would have you to be circumcised’ (6:12–13). Peter’s fault at Antioch was withdrawing from table fellowship with Gentiles (2:12). Paul is fighting the idea that Gentiles must become Jews, not the idea that Christians must love their neighbour."],
  ["Paul makes conduct decisive in the same letter","“The works of the flesh are manifest, which are fornication, uncleanness… envies, murders, drunkenness… of the which I foretell you, as I have foretold to you, that they who do such things shall not obtain the kingdom of God” (5:19–21). “What things a man shall sow, those also shall he reap. For he that soweth in his flesh, of the flesh also shall reap corruption. But he that soweth in the spirit, of the spirit shall reap life everlasting” (6:7–8). “In doing good, let us not fail. For in due time we shall reap, not failing” (6:9). That is not ‘faith alone’."],
  ["The Catholic sacraments are not ‘works of the law’","Baptism is Christ’s own command (Matthew 28:19), and Paul in this very letter says: ‘as many of you as have been baptized in Christ have put on Christ’ (3:27). Paul treats baptism as the means by which we are clothed with Christ, not as a rival to faith. The sacraments are Christ acting, not human achievements."],
  ["Faith working through charity","Galatians 5:6 is the key to the letter, and it is the Catholic formula. Trent: through justification, faith, hope and charity are infused, ‘for faith, unless hope and charity be added thereto, neither unites man perfectly with Christ, nor makes him a living member of his body’ (Session 6, ch. 7). Thomas calls this ‘formed faith’ (fides formata), faith given its form by charity (ST II-II q.4 a.3)."],
  ["‘Another gospel’ cuts both ways","Paul anathematizes a gospel ‘besides that which we have preached’ (1:8), which the Galatians had ‘received’ (1:9, parelabete, the word for receiving tradition). The question is which community has kept the gospel as first received. The Catholic Church can show continuous teaching on baptism, the Eucharist and the necessity of charity from the first century; sola fide as formulated in 1520s Wittenberg cannot."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Jerome, Commentary on Galatians (386)","Reads ‘works of the law’ as the Jewish ceremonies: circumcision, Sabbath, new moons, food laws (paraphrased)."],
  ["John Chrysostom, Homilies on Galatians (c. 395)","The Galatians were not denying Christ but adding circumcision as necessary, which overturned grace (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Galatians, ch. 5, lect. 2","On 5:6: the faith that justifies is faith formed by charity; unformed faith, without charity, is dead (paraphrased)."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 6, ch. 7","Quotes Galatians 5:6: faith that works by charity, with hope and charity infused, makes us living members of Christ."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Paul says if righteousness came by the law Christ died in vain (2:21). Catholics make Christ’s death insufficient.”","Catholics say Christ’s death is the sole meritorious cause of our justification (Trent, Session 6, ch. 7). What we add is not to Christ’s merits but our acceptance of them, which is itself grace. Paul’s own words: ‘I live, now not I, but Christ liveth in me’ (2:20). Christ living in us produces works; that is the point."],
  ["“Galatians 3:10: ‘as many as are of the works of the law are under a curse’. Any reliance on works brings a curse.”","Paul’s proof (3:10) quotes Deuteronomy 27:26: cursed is everyone who does not keep everything in the Law. The curse falls on those who rely on the Mosaic covenant as their charter of righteousness. Christ ‘redeemed us from the curse of the law’ (3:13) so that ‘the blessing of Abraham might come on the Gentiles’ (3:14). The contrast is Torah versus Christ, not obedience versus faith."]],
 talk="“Galatians is about circumcision. False teachers were telling Gentile Christians they had to become Jews to be saved, and Paul says no: the Law of Moses doesn’t justify anyone. Catholics agree. But the same letter says people who live in fornication, drunkenness and hatred won’t inherit the kingdom (5:21), and that what counts is ‘faith working through love’ (5:6). That’s the Catholic position in Paul’s own words.”",
 avoid="Do not say Paul is ‘only’ talking about Jewish identity markers as if he had no wider principle; he also teaches that no one can earn grace by human effort, which Catholics affirm. Do not equate Catholic devotions with the Mosaic Law; make the distinction clearly.",
 read=["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Galatians, chs. 2, 3, 5","Council of Trent, Session 6, ch. 7","CCC 1812–1816, 1992","Scott Hahn (2002) A Father Who Keeps His Promises. Cincinnati: Servant, chs. 8–9"])

D('romans',4,5,
 ctx="Romans 4 uses Abraham to prove that righteousness comes by faith. “Abraham believed God, and it was reputed to him unto justice” (4:3, quoting Genesis 15:6). Verses 4–5: “Now to him that worketh, the reward is not reckoned according to grace, but according to debt. But to him that worketh not, yet believeth in him that justifieth the ungodly, his faith is reputed to justice, according to the purpose of the grace of God.” Then Paul quotes David (Psalm 31:1–2, ‘Blessed are they whose iniquities are forgiven’) and shows that Abraham was reckoned righteous before his circumcision (4:9–12).",
 who="This verse is the centre of the Reformed doctrine of ‘imputed righteousness’: justification is a forensic (legal) declaration. God, as judge, counts Christ’s righteousness to the believer’s account. The believer is not made inwardly righteous by justification; he is declared righteous while remaining a sinner. Luther’s phrase: simul iustus et peccator, at once righteous and a sinner. Sanctification (actual inward change) follows as a separate process but contributes nothing to our standing before God.\n\nThe strongest form: ‘The verb logizomai is an accounting term, “to credit to an account”. God justifies “the ungodly”, so justification cannot be a description of what we already are; it is a verdict credited to us.’",
 lang="Logizomai (‘to reckon, account, credit’) appears 11 times in Romans 4. The Greek translation of Genesis 15:6 uses it, and so does Psalm 105:31 (106:31 in Hebrew numbering), of Phinehas: his zealous act ‘was reputed to him unto justice’. The same formula is used of a deed, not only of faith.\n\n“Justifieth” (dikaiounta) is present tense: God is the one who makes righteous. In Hebrew, ‘justify’ (hitsdiq) can mean to declare righteous in court; but when God speaks, his word accomplishes what it says (Isaiah 55:11).",
 steps=[
  ["God’s declaration is creative, not fictional","When a human judge declares a guilty man innocent, the declaration is false (Proverbs 17:15: ‘he that justifieth the wicked… is abominable before God’). God cannot declare something true that is not. When God declares, it comes to be: ‘God said: Be light made. And light was made’ (Genesis 1:3). So God’s verdict ‘righteous’ makes the sinner righteous. The Catholic Church agrees justification is God’s declaration; it denies that the declaration is a legal fiction."],
  ["Paul describes justification as transformation","Within the same letter: ‘the charity of God is poured forth in our hearts, by the Holy Ghost, who is given to us’ (5:5); ‘by the obedience of one, many shall be made just’ (5:19, katastathēsontai, ‘constituted’); ‘we are buried together with him by baptism into death… that we also may walk in newness of life’ (6:4); ‘he that is dead is justified from sin’ (6:7). Elsewhere: ‘you are washed, you are sanctified, you are justified’ (1 Corinthians 6:11); ‘if any be in Christ a new creature’ (2 Corinthians 5:17)."],
  ["‘To him that worketh not’ means the one working for wages","Verse 4 defines the ‘worker’: one to whom the reward is reckoned ‘according to debt’, as wages owed. Paul excludes the idea that we put God in our debt. Catholics agree: no one can merit the first grace (Trent, Session 6, ch. 8). He does not say that the justified person does nothing; he goes on to say the justified must walk in newness of life (6:4) and be ‘servants of justice’ (6:18)."],
  ["Abraham was ‘justified’ more than once","Abraham had faith and obeyed God long before Genesis 15: ‘By faith Abraham… obeyed to go out into a place which he was to receive for an inheritance’ (Hebrews 11:8, referring to Genesis 12). Genesis 15:6 is a later moment, and James 2:21–23 sees Genesis 22, the offering of Isaac, as the fulfilment of 15:6. So Scripture presents Abraham’s righteousness as growing through faith and obedience over his life: exactly the Catholic doctrine of increase in justification (Trent, Session 6, ch. 10)."],
  ["The same ‘reckoning’ formula is used of an act","Psalm 105:30–31: Phinehas ‘stood up and pacified’ God, ‘and it was reputed to him unto justice, to generation and generation for evermore’. If ‘reckoned as righteousness’ meant crediting an alien righteousness instead of a real one, it could not describe a deed. It means that God recognizes and rewards true righteousness, which he himself has worked."],
  ["Forgiveness removes sin; it does not merely cover it","Paul quotes Psalm 31: ‘whose sins are covered’. Protestants read ‘covered’ as hidden but still present. But Scripture uses many images: sins are ‘blotted out’ (Isaiah 43:25; Acts 3:19), ‘washed’ (Psalm 50:4, 9: ‘thou shalt wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow’), removed ‘as far as the east is from the west’ (Psalm 102:12). Covering is one image among several, and the others show that the stain is truly taken away."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, On the Spirit and the Letter 26.45 (412)","‘Justified’ means ‘made just’: ‘what else is “being justified” than being made just by him who justifies the ungodly, so that from ungodly he becomes just?’ (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST I-II q.113 a.1 and q.110 a.2","Justification is a movement from the state of injustice to the state of justice; grace is a real quality in the soul, not only God’s favour."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 6, ch. 7 and canon 11 (1547)","We are ‘not only reputed, but are truly called, and are, just, receiving justice within us’. Condemns the claim that we are justified ‘by the sole imputation of the justice of Christ… to the exclusion of the grace and charity which is poured forth in their hearts by the Holy Ghost’."],
  ["Joint Declaration on Justification (1999), §§22–24","Catholics and Lutherans agree that God both forgives sin and frees from its power; they differ on how to describe the remaining concupiscence."]],
 obj=[
  ["“If justification makes us inwardly righteous, why do Christians still sin?”","Because concupiscence, the inclination to sin, remains after baptism for the struggle (Trent, Session 5, canon 5; CCC 1264, 1426). Concupiscence is not itself sin unless consented to. Venial sins do not destroy grace. Justification is real but not yet complete in glory; that is why it can grow (2 Peter 3:18) and why we pray ‘forgive us our trespasses’."],
  ["“Imputation protects assurance: if my standing depends on inner change, I can never be sure.”","The firm ground of hope is God’s faithfulness, not my self-inspection (Romans 8:31–39; Hebrews 6:18–19). Catholics have a confident hope, not an anxious calculation. But Scripture itself warns that the justified can fall (Romans 11:22; 1 Corinthians 10:12), which pure imputation cannot easily explain."],
  ["“Christ’s righteousness is credited to us (2 Corinthians 5:21).”","‘That we might be made (genōmetha, become) the justice of God in him.’ Paul says ‘become’, not ‘be credited with’. Catholics affirm that Christ’s merits are the meritorious cause of our justification; what we receive is a real sharing in God’s righteousness (2 Peter 1:4, ‘partakers of the divine nature’)."]],
 talk="“When God says something, it happens. ‘Let there be light’ and there was light. So when God declares a sinner righteous, he doesn’t pretend; he makes it true. That’s why Paul, a few verses later, says God’s love is poured into our hearts by the Holy Spirit (Romans 5:5) and that through Christ many are made righteous (5:19). Catholics believe justification is God’s verdict, and it’s a verdict that changes us, not a paper transaction.”",
 avoid="Do not say Catholics reject the legal or forensic dimension; Trent accepts that justification includes the forgiveness of sins. The disagreement is whether it is only that. Do not caricature imputation as ‘snow-covered dung’ and attribute the phrase to Luther; it is not found in his writings.",
 read=["Council of Trent, Session 6, chs. 7, 10, canons 10–11","CCC 1987–1995","ST I-II q.113","Augustine, On the Spirit and the Letter","John Henry Newman (1838) Lectures on Justification","Michael Bird (2007) The Saving Righteousness of God. Milton Keynes: Paternoster (an evangelical scholar who questions strict imputation)"])

D('romans',10,9,
 ctx="Romans 9–11 deals with Israel’s unbelief. In 10:5–13 Paul contrasts the righteousness of the Law with the righteousness of faith, quoting Deuteronomy 30:14: ‘The word is nigh thee, even in thy mouth, and in thy heart.’ Then: “For if thou confess with thy mouth the Lord Jesus, and believe in thy heart that God hath raised him up from the dead, thou shalt be saved. For, with the heart, we believe unto justice; but, with the mouth, confession is made unto salvation” (10:9–10). He quotes Joel 2:32: ‘Whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord shall be saved’ (10:13), and then asks how people can call without preachers who are sent (10:14–15).",
 who="Evangelical altar calls and the ‘sinner’s prayer’ rest heavily on this verse: if you sincerely say ‘Jesus is Lord’ and believe in the resurrection, you are saved, at that moment, permanently. Baptism, Church membership and sacraments are good but not part of salvation itself.",
 lang="“Confess” (homologeō) means to agree publicly, to declare allegiance. ‘Jesus is Lord’ (Kyrios Iēsous) was the earliest baptismal creed (1 Corinthians 12:3; Philippians 2:11). In a Roman context, where ‘Caesar is Lord’ was the civic confession, it was a dangerous public act of allegiance, often sealed with martyrdom.\n\n“Shall be saved” (sōthēsē) is future tense.",
 steps=[
  ["Paul describes saving faith; he does not offer a formula","Nowhere in the New Testament is a convert told to pray a prayer and then be assured of salvation. The ‘sinner’s prayer’ as an evangelistic technique dates from 18th- and 19th-century revivalism (popularized by preachers such as Charles Finney and, in the 20th century, Billy Graham’s crusades). Paul’s point is that salvation is near and simple: faith in the heart, confession with the lips. Catholics fully agree."],
  ["Confession of ‘Jesus is Lord’ was made at baptism","In the early Church the creed was professed at baptism (Acts 8:37 in some manuscripts: ‘I believe that Jesus Christ is the Son of God’; the Didache and Hippolytus’ Apostolic Tradition describe baptismal professions). Paul’s readers would hear 10:9 as describing what they did when they were baptized, a few chapters after he has taught that we are ‘buried together with him by baptism into death’ (6:4)."],
  ["Calling on the name of the Lord, in Acts, includes baptism","Paul is told: ‘Rise up, and be baptized, and wash away thy sins, invoking his name’ (Acts 22:16). Peter at Pentecost quotes the same Joel prophecy (Acts 2:21) and then answers ‘what shall we do?’ with ‘Do penance, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ, for the remission of your sins’ (2:38). Calling on the name and being baptized go together."],
  ["Confession of Christ is lifelong","Jesus: ‘Every one therefore that shall confess me before men, I will also confess him before my Father’ (Matthew 10:32), and ‘he that shall persevere unto the end, he shall be saved’ (10:22). A confession once made and later denied by one’s life does not save: ‘They profess that they know God: but in their works they deny him’ (Titus 1:16). ‘Not every one that saith to me, Lord, Lord, shall enter into the kingdom of heaven’ (Matthew 7:21)."],
  ["Paul immediately points to the Church’s mission","10:14–15: ‘How shall they believe him, of whom they have not heard? And how shall they hear, without a preacher? And how shall they preach unless they be sent?’ Faith comes through the preaching of those sent by Christ (10:17). The verse leads straight into the apostolic Church, not away from it."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Hippolytus (attributed), Apostolic Tradition 21 (c. 215)","Describes baptism in Rome: the candidate is asked ‘Do you believe in Christ Jesus, the Son of God…?’, answers ‘I believe’, and is immersed after each profession."],
  ["Cyril of Jerusalem, Catechetical Lectures 5.12 (c. 350)","The creed is taught to catechumens to be confessed with the mouth and kept in the heart (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Romans, ch. 10, lect. 2","Interior faith is enough for justification in the heart, but outward confession is necessary for salvation when occasion demands it, as with the martyrs (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“The verse doesn’t mention baptism, so it isn’t necessary.”","Romans 10:9 doesn’t mention repentance either, yet everyone agrees repentance is necessary. Scripture must be read whole. Mark 16:16, John 3:5, Acts 2:38, Romans 6:3–4 and 1 Peter 3:21 all speak of baptism."],
  ["“I prayed the sinner’s prayer sincerely, so I know I’m saved.”","Rejoice that you came to Christ: that was grace. Catholics would say the next step Christ himself asks is baptism into his Church, if you have not received valid baptism, and a life of faithful discipleship (Matthew 28:19–20). Most Protestant baptisms are valid and recognized by the Catholic Church (CCC 1271)."]],
 talk="“Romans 10:9 is beautiful and true: faith in the heart and confessing Jesus as Lord is how salvation begins. In the early Church, that confession was made out loud at baptism; that’s why Acts 22:16 says ‘be baptized and wash away your sins, calling on his name’. And Jesus said not everyone who says ‘Lord, Lord’ will enter the kingdom, but whoever does the Father’s will. So the confession is a beginning, and it has to be lived out.”",
 avoid="Do not mock people who came to faith through a sinner’s prayer; God uses it. Do not say evangelicals ‘aren’t saved’; the Church recognizes valid baptism and real faith among them (CCC 818, 838).",
 read=["CCC 185–197 (the creed), 1229–1233 (Christian initiation)","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Romans, ch. 10","Hippolytus (attributed), Apostolic Tradition 21"])

D('1-john',5,13,
 ctx="1 John is written to reassure a community shaken by a schism: some had ‘gone out from us’ (2:19), denying that Jesus Christ came in the flesh (4:2–3). John gives ‘tests’ by which believers can know they are in the truth: keeping the commandments (2:3–5), loving the brethren (3:14), confessing Christ come in the flesh (4:2), the Spirit’s witness (4:13). Then 5:13: “These things I write to you, that you may know that you have eternal life, you who believe in the name of the Son of God.” A few verses later: “He that knoweth his brother to sin a sin which is not to death, let him ask, and life shall be given to him… There is a sin unto death: for that I say not that any man ask” (5:16).",
 who="This verse is the main text for ‘assurance of salvation’: believers can know with certainty, now, that they are going to heaven. In Calvinist form this is tied to ‘perseverance of the saints’ (the truly elect cannot fall away); in Baptist and evangelical form, ‘once saved, always saved’ (eternal security). Catholics, who say no one can be absolutely certain of final salvation without special revelation, are accused of robbing believers of the joy John wants them to have.",
 lang="“That you may know” (hina eidēte): eidō, ‘know’ in the sense of being aware, assured. John uses ‘know’ (ginōskō and eidō) over 30 times in the letter, almost always with a condition: ‘by this we know that we have known him, if we keep his commandments’ (2:3); ‘in this we know that we are in him’ (2:5).\n\n“You have eternal life” (zōēn echete aiōnion), present tense: eternal life is a present possession, the life of grace (John 17:3).",
 steps=[
  ["Catholics affirm a real, joyful confidence","The Church teaches the virtue of hope: a ‘firm’ and ‘certain’ trust in God’s promises (CCC 1817–1821; Hebrews 6:19, ‘an anchor of the soul, sure and firm’). Trent says no one should doubt God’s mercy, Christ’s merits or the power of the sacraments (Session 6, ch. 9). What Trent rejects is the claim that a believer must be certain, with the certainty of faith, that he himself is predestined and will persevere, as if this were part of saving faith (canons 15–16)."],
  ["John’s assurance is conditional on a living faith","In the same letter: ‘He that saith he knoweth him, and keepeth not his commandments, is a liar’ (2:4). ‘Whosoever hateth his brother is a murderer. And you know that no murderer hath eternal life abiding in himself’ (3:15). ‘If we walk in the light… the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin’ (1:7). You can know you have eternal life now, if you believe, love and keep the commandments. That is exactly Catholic teaching: a person in the state of grace has eternal life and can know it with moral certainty."],
  ["Having eternal life now is not the same as being unable to lose it","Eternal life is the life of God in the soul. It can be killed by grave sin. John himself says there is ‘a sin unto death’ (5:16), committed by a ‘brother’, that is, a Christian. Jesus: ‘If any one abide not in me, he shall be cast forth as a branch, and shall wither’ (John 15:6). Paul: ‘Wherefore he that thinketh himself to stand, let him take heed lest he fall’ (1 Corinthians 10:12)."],
  ["Paul did not claim absolute certainty about himself","‘I am not conscious to myself of any thing, yet am I not hereby justified; but he that judgeth me, is the Lord’ (1 Corinthians 4:4). ‘I chastise my body, and bring it into subjection: lest perhaps, when I have preached to others, I myself should become reprobate’ (9:27, adokimos). ‘Not as though I had already attained… but I follow after’ (Philippians 3:12). Near the end of his life, with the race finished, he says ‘there is laid up for me a crown of justice’ (2 Timothy 4:7–8)."],
  ["Mortal and venial sin come from this very chapter","1 John 5:16–17 distinguishes ‘sin unto death’ and ‘sin not unto death’: ‘All iniquity is sin. And there is a sin unto death.’ This is a scriptural root of the Catholic distinction between mortal and venial sin (CCC 1854). A believer can know he is in grace (no unrepented mortal sin) and has eternal life, while humbly entrusting final perseverance to God."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, On Rebuke and Grace 13.40 (426–427)","No one can be certain that he is among the predestined while in this life, lest pride creep in; ‘let him that thinketh himself to stand take heed lest he fall’ (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST I-II q.112 a.5","No one can know with certainty that he has grace, except by special revelation; but one can know it conjecturally by signs: delight in God, contempt of worldly things, not being conscious of mortal sin."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 6, chs. 9, 12, 13; canons 15–16","Against vain confidence; no one can know with the certainty of faith that he is predestined, except by special revelation; perseverance is a ‘great gift’ to be hoped for and prayed for."],
  ["CCC 2005, 1821","We cannot be certain by our feelings, but reflection on God’s blessings gives assurance that grace is at work; we can and should hope for final perseverance."]],
 obj=[
  ["“If I can’t be 100% sure, I’ll live in fear.”","Catholics live in hope, which casts out servile fear (1 John 4:18). A child who knows his father loves him is not terrified, even though he knows he could run away. The Catholic’s confidence rests on Christ, the sacraments (especially confession) and God’s faithfulness, not on calculation."],
  ["“Someone who falls away was never truly saved (1 John 2:19).”","2:19 describes the specific schismatics who left, ‘that they may be manifest, that they are not all of us’. It is not a general rule that every apostate was never a believer. Hebrews 6:4–6 and 2 Peter 2:20–22 describe people who were truly enlightened, ‘made partakers of the Holy Ghost’, ‘escaped the pollutions of the world’, and then fell. And the ‘never truly saved’ answer destroys assurance far more: no one can know he is not a false believer until he dies."],
  ["“John says ‘know’, not ‘hope’.”","Yes: you can know you have eternal life now, if you are believing and obeying. Catholic confession gives exactly that knowledge. What John does not say is that you can know you will never fall."]],
 talk="“I love that verse: John wants Christians to be confident. Catholics can be confident too: if you trust Christ, are keeping his commandments and loving your neighbour, you have eternal life right now. But read the whole letter: John also says whoever says he knows God but doesn’t keep his commandments is a liar, and that there’s a ‘sin unto death’ a Christian can commit. So the confidence is real, but it’s the confidence of a child walking with his Father, not a ticket you can’t lose.”",
 avoid="Do not tell anyone Catholics ‘can never know’ if they are in grace; Thomas and the Catechism say we can know by signs and should have confident hope. Do not imply Protestants all teach the same thing; Methodists and many Pentecostals reject ‘once saved, always saved’ too.",
 read=["Council of Trent, Session 6, chs. 9–13, canons 15–16, 23","ST I-II q.112 a.5","CCC 1817–1821, 1854–1864, 2005","Augustine, On Rebuke and Grace; On the Gift of Perseverance"])

D('philippians',2,12,
 ctx="Philippians 2:5–11 is the great hymn of Christ’s self-emptying and exaltation. Paul immediately applies it: “Wherefore, my dearly beloved, (as you have always obeyed, not as in my presence only, but much more now in my absence,) with fear and trembling work out your salvation. For it is God who worketh in you, both to will and to accomplish, according to his good will” (2:12–13). Then: ‘Do ye all things without murmurings… that you may be blameless’ (2:14–15), ‘holding forth the word of life to my glory in the day of Christ, because I have not run in vain’ (2:16).",
 who="This is a verse Catholics usually quote, and Protestants answer it. The typical Protestant reading: ‘work out’ means ‘express outwardly’ or ‘bring to completion in daily life’ a salvation already fully possessed; it is about sanctification, not justification, and verse 13 shows God does it all. Some also say ‘salvation’ here means the health of the community, not individual eternal salvation.",
 lang="“Work out” (katergazesthe) means to accomplish, bring about, carry through to completion (the same verb in Romans 7:18, ‘to accomplish that which is good’). It is present imperative, plural: keep on working it out, all of you.\n\n“Fear and trembling” (phobou kai tromou) is used for reverent awe in God’s presence (Psalm 2:11; 1 Corinthians 2:3; Ephesians 6:5).\n\n“God who worketh in you” (ho energōn): the same root as ‘energy’. God is energizing your willing and working.",
 steps=[
  ["Both verbs are real: we work because God works","Verse 12 commands our action; verse 13 grounds it in God’s action. Paul does not say ‘do nothing, because God does everything’, nor ‘God does part and you do part’. He says God works in us both the willing and the doing, so we can and must work. This is precisely the Catholic doctrine of grace and cooperation: grace does not compete with our freedom, it creates and frees it (CCC 2001–2002; Trent, Session 6, ch. 5)."],
  ["‘Salvation’ in Paul includes final salvation","Paul speaks of salvation as future and not yet completed: ‘now our salvation is nearer than when we believed’ (Romans 13:11); ‘he that shall persevere to the end, he shall be saved’ (Matthew 24:13). In Philippians, Paul hopes ‘if by any means I may attain to the resurrection which is from the dead’ (3:11) and says he has ‘not already attained’ (3:12). The ‘day of Christ’ (2:16) is the final judgment."],
  ["‘Fear and trembling’ would make no sense if nothing were at stake","If salvation were already secured beyond any possibility of loss, why fear and tremble? The awe is the right attitude of a creature who knows that God is at work in him and that he can resist that work. ‘Be not highminded, but fear’ (Romans 11:20)."],
  ["Perseverance is God’s gift and our task","Augustine and Trent both teach that final perseverance is a gift that cannot be merited, which God gives to those he will, and which we should pray for constantly (Trent, Session 6, ch. 13). This verse unites both sides: work with fear and trembling, because it is God who works in you."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, Sermon 169, 11.13 (c. 416)","‘He who made you without you will not justify you without you. He made you without your knowing it; he justifies you with your willing it’ (paraphrased translation)."],
  ["Augustine, On Grace and Free Will 16.32 (426–427)","It is certain that we will when we will, but he makes us will; it is certain that we act when we act, but he makes us act (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Philippians, ch. 2, lect. 3","We are to work out salvation with fear, both because of our own weakness and because of the danger; yet not with despair, since God works in us (paraphrased)."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 6, ch. 13","Quotes this verse: let those who think they stand take heed lest they fall, and with fear and trembling work out their salvation."]],
 obj=[
  ["“It means working out what is already worked in, like working out a math problem already solved.”","‘Work out’ (katergazomai) does mean bring to completion, and Catholics agree that what God begins he means to complete (Philippians 1:6). But completion is not automatic; the command is addressed to their freedom, with ‘fear and trembling’, ‘that you may be blameless’ (2:15), ‘that I have not run in vain’ (2:16). Paul could ‘run in vain’ if they did not persevere."],
  ["“This is about sanctification, not justification.”","The Reformation distinction between justification (a once-for-all verdict) and sanctification (lifelong change) is not how Paul uses his vocabulary. He can speak of being ‘sanctified’ as past (1 Corinthians 6:11) and justification as future (Romans 2:13; Galatians 5:5, ‘we wait for the hope of justice’). Salvation includes both."]],
 talk="“Paul says, ‘work out your salvation with fear and trembling, for it is God who works in you both to will and to do.’ Notice both halves. God does all of it, and we really do it, because he’s working through our freedom, not around it. Catholics don’t think we earn salvation. We think grace makes us able to cooperate, and that we have to keep cooperating until the end, with reverent fear, but without despair.”",
 avoid="Do not use this verse alone to prove salvation by works; verse 13 is part of the sentence. Quote them together.",
 read=["CCC 2001–2002, 2016","Council of Trent, Session 6, chs. 5, 13","Augustine, On Grace and Free Will","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Philippians, ch. 2"])

D('hebrews',6,4,
 ctx="Hebrews is written to Jewish Christians tempted to drift back from Christ to the old Temple system under pressure (10:32–39). In 5:11–6:3 the author rebukes them for spiritual immaturity. Then the warning: “For it is impossible for those who were once illuminated, have tasted also the heavenly gift, and were made partakers of the Holy Ghost, have moreover tasted the good word of God, and the powers of the world to come, and are fallen away: to be renewed again to penance, crucifying again to themselves the Son of God, and making him a mockery” (6:4–6). Verses 7–8: land that drinks the rain and bears thorns ‘is reprobate, and very near unto a curse’. Verse 9: ‘But… we trust better things of you.’",
 who="This is a passage Catholics (and Arminian Protestants) use against eternal security. Calvinists and Baptists answer in three main ways: (1) the people described were never truly regenerated; they only ‘tasted’; (2) the passage is hypothetical (‘if they fell away, which cannot happen’); (3) the loss is of rewards, not salvation. Separately, the verse was historically used by rigorists (Montanists, Novatianists) to claim that Christians who apostatize cannot be forgiven.",
 lang="“Once illuminated” (hapax phōtisthentas): ‘illumination’ (phōtismos) was an early Christian name for baptism (Justin Martyr, First Apology 61, c. 155).\n\n“Partakers of the Holy Ghost” (metochous pneumatos hagiou): the same word metochos is used in 3:14, ‘we are made partakers (metochoi) of Christ, yet so if we hold the beginning of his substance firm unto the end’, a real participation.\n\n“Tasted” (geusamenous): the same verb is used of Christ ‘tasting death for all’ (2:9). Tasting in Hebrews means real experience, not a sample.\n\n“Fallen away” (parapesontas) is an aorist participle in the same series as the others: it is not grammatically ‘if they fall away’; it simply describes them.",
 steps=[
  ["The description is of real Christians","Every phrase describes genuine Christian experience: baptismal illumination, receiving the heavenly gift (the Eucharist or grace), sharing in the Holy Spirit, experiencing the word and miracles. The same author uses ‘partakers’ for real union with Christ (3:14) and ‘tasted’ for Christ’s real death (2:9). If these are unregenerate people, words have no meaning. Hebrews’ whole purpose is to warn real believers against apostasy (2:1–3; 3:12–14; 10:26–31; 12:15–17)."],
  ["So a true believer can fall away","That is the plain sense, and the consistent teaching of the Catholic Church (Trent, Session 6, canon 23 condemns the claim that a justified person cannot lose grace). The whole of Hebrews 3 builds on Israel in the desert: they were truly delivered from Egypt and still ‘their bodies were overthrown in the desert’ (1 Corinthians 10:5)."],
  ["‘Impossible to renew to penance’ does not mean God cannot forgive","Read in context, the author says it is impossible for them to be renewed while they keep on crucifying Christ (anastaurountas, present participle: ‘while they are crucifying’). As long as someone persists in repudiating Christ, the only sacrifice that saves, there is nothing else to turn to (10:26: ‘there is now left no sacrifice for sins’). The Church condemned the Novatianist reading that apostates can never be reconciled; Peter himself denied Christ three times and was restored (John 21:15–17)."],
  ["The Fathers read ‘renewal’ as re-baptism","Many Fathers and Thomas read ‘impossible to renew’ as: it is impossible to be baptized a second time. Baptism is given once (Ephesians 4:5); the sinner after baptism is restored by penance, the ‘second plank after shipwreck’ (Tertullian; Jerome; Trent, Session 14, ch. 2)."],
  ["The ‘hypothetical’ reading empties the warning","A warning about something that cannot happen is no warning. And verse 9, ‘we trust better things of you’, assumes the readers could go either way; the author is hopeful, not certain. 10:38–39: ‘if he withdraw himself, he shall not please my soul. But we are not the children of withdrawing unto perdition.’"]],
 fathers=[
  ["Justin Martyr, First Apology 61 (c. 155)","‘This washing is called illumination, because they who learn these things are illuminated in their understandings.’"],
  ["John Chrysostom, Homilies on Hebrews 9 (c. 400)","Explains ‘impossible to renew’ as: there is no second baptism; but repentance is still possible (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Hebrews, ch. 6, lect. 1","Impossible to be renewed by baptism again, since baptism configures to Christ’s death, which happened once; but not impossible to be restored by penance (paraphrased)."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 6, ch. 14 and canon 23; Session 14, ch. 2","The justified can lose grace by mortal sin and recover it by the sacrament of penance."]],
 obj=[
  ["“They only tasted; they never really ate.”","Hebrews 2:9: Jesus ‘tasted death for all’. Did he only sample death? The word means to experience fully."],
  ["“John 10:28 says no one can snatch Christ’s sheep.”","No outside power can. But the sheep can stop following (10:27, ‘my sheep… follow me’, present tense). Hebrews warns about our own unbelief: ‘lest perhaps there be in any of you an evil heart of unbelief, to depart from the living God’ (3:12)."],
  ["“If Christians can fall, salvation depends on us.”","It depends on God, who gives both the grace and the perseverance; but God does not save us against our will. A gift can be rejected. That is why Hebrews tells us to ‘hold fast the confession of our hope without wavering’ (10:23)."]],
 talk="“Look at how Hebrews describes these people: they’ve been enlightened, tasted the heavenly gift, shared in the Holy Spirit, tasted God’s word and the powers of the age to come. That’s a Christian. And then they fall away. The writer isn’t speaking of fake believers; he’s warning real ones. Catholics read it that way: a Christian can walk away from Christ. The good news is that as long as we’re alive, we can come back, like Peter did after denying him.”",
 avoid="Do not use this verse to suggest apostates can never return; that was the Novatianist heresy. Do not suggest Calvinists ‘don’t care’ about holiness; they argue perseverance shows itself in holiness.",
 read=["Council of Trent, Session 6, chs. 12–15, canon 23; Session 14, chs. 1–2","CCC 1446, 1861","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Hebrews, ch. 6","Scot McKnight (1992) ‘The Warning Passages of Hebrews’, Trinity Journal 13, 21–59 (an evangelical scholar who reads them as real warnings)"])

# ---------------- New entries with full studies ----------------
add('john',3,16,'John 3:16','"Whosoever believeth in him may not perish"',
 'The best-known verse in the Bible is read as the whole gospel in one sentence: believe in Jesus and you will have eternal life. Nothing else (baptism, sacraments, works, the Church) is part of the condition.',
 ['In John, "believe" (pisteuō) is never bare assent. The same chapter ends: "He that believeth in the Son hath life everlasting; but he that believeth not (apeithōn, literally "disobeys") the Son shall not see life" (3:36). Believing and obeying are parallel.',
  'The same conversation began with baptism: "Unless a man be born again of water and the Holy Ghost, he cannot enter into the kingdom of God" (3:5). Right afterwards Jesus and his disciples go out baptizing (3:22).',
  'Verses 19–21 explain who believes: those who "do truth" come to the light; those whose "works were evil" love darkness.',
  'Catholics believe John 3:16 completely. The question is what "believe" means in John, and John answers: a faith that comes to the light, obeys, and is born of water and the Spirit.'],
 'God\'s love is the source of everything. Believing in the Son means entrusting your whole life to him: born of water and the Spirit, walking in the light, obeying him.',
 'CCC 219, 444, 1215, 1814; John 3:5, 3:36; 14:15, 21',
 tags=('salvation','baptism'))
D('john',3,16,
 ctx="John 3:1–21 records Jesus’ night conversation with Nicodemus, a Pharisee and member of the Sanhedrin. Jesus begins: ‘unless a man be born again (anōthen, also ‘from above’), he cannot see the kingdom of God’ (3:3), and explains: ‘Unless a man be born again of water and the Holy Ghost, he cannot enter into the kingdom of God’ (3:5). He speaks of the Son of Man being ‘lifted up’ like the bronze serpent (3:14–15). Then 3:16–21, which may be Jesus’ words or John’s commentary: God’s love, the Son given, believing, judgment, light and darkness, doing truth. The chapter closes with John the Baptist and the line: ‘He that believeth not the Son shall not see life’ (3:36).",
 who="John 3:16 is often presented as the gospel in miniature: the only condition for eternal life is to believe. Since Catholicism adds sacraments, obedience and Church membership, it is said to add conditions God did not set.",
 lang="“Believeth” (ho pisteuōn) is a present participle: ‘the one who keeps believing’. John uses the verb ‘believe’ 98 times but never the noun ‘faith’ (pistis); for John, believing is an ongoing activity.\n\n3:36 contrasts ho pisteuōn (the one believing) with ho apeithōn (the one disobeying). Apeitheō means ‘refuse to be persuaded, disobey’; the Douay ‘believeth not’ follows the Vulgate (incredulus), but the Greek pairs faith with obedience.\n\n“Only begotten” (monogenēs): unique, one of a kind; used in John 1:14, 18 of the eternal Son.",
 steps=[
  ["Catholics affirm the verse without reservation","God’s love is the first cause of salvation; the Son’s gift on the Cross is its meritorious cause; faith is the beginning of salvation (CCC 458, 1814). There is nothing in John 3:16 a Catholic needs to explain away."],
  ["‘Believe’ in John is a whole-life commitment","Present tense: the one who goes on believing. In 3:36, the opposite of believing is disobeying. In 3:19–21 the believer is the one who ‘doth truth’ and comes to the light ‘that his works may be made manifest, because they are done in God’. Later: ‘If you love me, keep my commandments’ (14:15); ‘He that hath my commandments, and keepeth them; he it is that loveth me’ (14:21)."],
  ["The context is baptismal","Jesus’ teaching to Nicodemus begins with being born ‘of water and the Holy Ghost’ (3:5), and the narrative continues with Jesus’ disciples baptizing (3:22; 4:1–2). In the Fathers, almost without exception, 3:5 refers to baptism. To quote 3:16 against baptism is to tear it from its own chapter."],
  ["Some ‘believed’ in John and were not saved","‘Many of the chief men also believed in him; but because of the Pharisees they did not confess him, that they might not be cast out of the synagogue. For they loved the glory of men more than the glory of God’ (12:42–43). In 8:31 Jesus speaks to ‘those Jews, who believed him’ and within a few verses says they seek to kill him (8:37, 40). Belief that does not abide and obey is not the faith of 3:16 (8:31: ‘If you continue in my word, you shall be my disciples indeed’)."],
  ["One verse is not the whole gospel","The same Jesus says: ‘Except you eat the flesh of the Son of man, and drink his blood, you shall not have life in you’ (6:54); ‘whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them’ (20:23); ‘If thou wilt enter into life, keep the commandments’ (Matthew 19:17). Faith in Christ includes accepting these words too."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Justin Martyr, First Apology 61 (c. 155)","On baptism: ‘Christ also said, “Unless you be born again, you shall not enter into the kingdom of heaven.”’"],
  ["Augustine, Tractates on John 12.12 (c. 407)","The serpent lifted up heals those who look on it; so Christ crucified heals those who look on him in faith (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on John, ch. 3, lect. 3","Faith that gives eternal life is faith formed by charity; unformed faith does not save (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“You can’t make a simple verse complicated. A child can understand it.”","Yes, and a child understands that ‘believing’ in someone means trusting and obeying them. Catholics are not complicating it; we are reading it with the verses around it."],
  ["“Water in 3:5 means physical birth (amniotic fluid).”","That reading is unknown in the early Church. Jesus explains what ‘born from above’ means: born of water and the Spirit, which at once evokes Ezekiel 36:25–27 (‘I will pour upon you clean water… and I will put a new spirit within you’) and the baptism John the Baptist promised (Mark 1:8). And immediately afterwards they go out baptizing (3:22)."]],
 talk="“John 3:16 is the heart of everything: God so loved the world he gave his Son. Catholics believe every word. But in John, ‘believe’ is a lifelong trust that obeys. Twenty verses later John says ‘whoever disobeys the Son will not see life’. And the conversation started with Jesus saying you must be born of water and the Spirit. So believing in Jesus includes getting baptized, following him, and keeping his word.”",
 avoid="Do not imply that quoting John 3:16 is wrong or shallow; it is the Church’s verse too. Avoid sarcasm about ‘John 3:16 signs’.",
 read=["CCC 1214–1216, 1257","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on John, ch. 3","Raymond E. Brown (1966) The Gospel according to John I–XII. Anchor Bible 29. New York: Doubleday, pp. 128–149"])

add('romans',5,1,'Romans 5:1','"Being justified by faith, let us have peace with God"',
 'The past tense "being justified" is taken to mean that justification is a completed, once-for-all event: the believer is permanently right with God and can never lose that peace.',
 ['Catholics agree that the baptized believer has been truly justified and has peace with God. That is the state of grace.',
  'The same chapter says justification is real inner change: "the charity of God is poured forth in our hearts by the Holy Ghost" (5:5); "by the obedience of one, many shall be made just" (5:19, future tense).',
  'Paul also speaks of justification as future: "the doers of the law shall be justified" (2:13); "we wait for the hope of justice" (Galatians 5:5).',
  'Peace can be lost by grave sin, as Romans 8:13 warns: "if you live according to the flesh, you shall die."'],
 'Justification is a real beginning, received in faith and baptism, that grows and must be kept until the end.',
 'Trent, Session 6, chs. 10, 14; CCC 1989–1995',
 tags=('justification','assurance'))
D('romans',5,1,
 ctx="Romans 5 opens Paul’s description of the life of the justified. ‘Being justified therefore by faith, let us have peace with God, through our Lord Jesus Christ: by whom also we have access through faith into this grace, wherein we stand, and glory in the hope of the glory of the sons of God’ (5:1–2). Tribulation produces patience, patience trial, trial hope (5:3–4), ‘and hope confoundeth not: because the charity of God is poured forth in our hearts, by the Holy Ghost, who is given to us’ (5:5). Then the Adam–Christ parallel (5:12–21).",
 who="Reformed theology reads the aorist participle ‘having been justified’ (dikaiōthentes) as a completed legal act that cannot be repeated, increased or lost. Justification is not a process; that is sanctification. So Catholic teaching on growth in justification and its loss by mortal sin contradicts the verse.",
 lang="Dikaiōthentes: aorist passive participle, ‘having been justified’. The aorist presents an action as a whole; it does not by itself mean ‘permanent’ or ‘unrepeatable’.\n\n“Let us have peace” (echōmen, subjunctive) is the reading of many of the best manuscripts and of the Vulgate (habeamus); others read echomen, ‘we have peace’. Either way, peace is something to be kept.\n\n“Grace, wherein we stand” (en hē hestēkamen, perfect): we have come to stand and are standing. 11:20: ‘thou standest by faith: be not highminded, but fear’.",
 steps=[
  ["Catholics affirm a real, past act of justification","At baptism, or when a convert comes to faith with the desire of baptism, he is truly justified: sins forgiven, grace infused (Trent, Session 6, ch. 7). Paul can rightly say ‘having been justified’. The disagreement is not whether justification happened, but whether it can grow or be lost."],
  ["The aorist does not mean ‘can never be lost’","Paul uses aorists of things that can be undone: the Galatians ‘began in the Spirit’ and are in danger of ending in the flesh (Galatians 3:3). Romans 11:22: ‘toward thee, the goodness of God, if thou abide in goodness, otherwise thou also shalt be cut off’. ‘Standing’ in grace (5:2) is what one can fall from (Galatians 5:4: ‘you are fallen from grace’)."],
  ["Justification in Romans has past, present and future dimensions","Past: 5:1, 9. Future: ‘the doers of the law shall be justified’ (2:13), ‘many shall be made just’ (5:19). Galatians 5:5: ‘we in spirit, by faith, wait for the hope of justice’. Catholic teaching gathers all of these: initial justification, its increase (Trent, Session 6, ch. 10: ‘he that is just, let him be justified still’, Revelation 22:11), and final justification at the judgment."],
  ["The peace of 5:1 is the peace of charity poured out","Paul defines what the justified have: access, hope, and ‘the charity of God poured forth in our hearts’ (5:5). This is the Catholic doctrine of sanctifying grace and infused charity. It is not only a change of status but a gift in the heart."]],
 fathers=[
  ["John Chrysostom, Homilies on Romans 9 (c. 390)","Reads ‘let us have peace’: having been reconciled, let us not return to sin and make war again (paraphrased)."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 6, ch. 10","The justified increase in that justice received through Christ’s grace, by faith cooperating with good works."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Growth is sanctification, not justification.”","The neat Reformation division is a later systematic distinction, not Paul’s vocabulary. In 1 Corinthians 6:11 sanctified comes before justified. The Catholic Church uses ‘justification’ for the whole movement from sin to righteousness, including growth."]],
 talk="“Yes, by faith and baptism we’ve truly been justified and have peace with God. Catholics believe that. But Paul also says that peace comes from God’s love poured into our hearts by the Holy Spirit (5:5), and that you can be ‘cut off’ if you don’t continue in God’s kindness (11:22). It’s like a marriage: it really happened on the wedding day, and it still has to be lived and can be betrayed.”",
 avoid="Do not argue from the variant reading ‘let us have peace’ as if it settled the matter; textual critics are divided.",
 read=["Council of Trent, Session 6, chs. 7, 10, 14–15","CCC 1987–1995, 1999–2000","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Romans, ch. 5, lect. 1"])

add('john',5,24,'John 5:24','"Hath life everlasting… is passed from death to life"',
 'The present tense "hath life everlasting" is taken to prove that eternal life, once received by faith, can never be lost: anything "eternal" cannot end.',
 ['"Eternal life" in John is the life of God shared with us now (17:3), and it can be killed. "No murderer hath eternal life abiding in himself" (1 John 3:15).',
  'The verse describes "he who heareth my word and believeth", present tense: the one who keeps hearing and believing.',
  'A few verses later Jesus says all will rise, "they that have done good things, unto the resurrection of life; but they that have done evil, unto the resurrection of judgment" (5:29).',
  'Jesus also warns believers that branches in him can be cut off (15:2, 6).'],
 'The one who believes possesses God\'s life now, and must abide in it to the end.',
 'CCC 1023–1029, 1861; John 15:1–10; 1 John 3:15',
 tags=('assurance',))
D('john',5,24,
 ctx="John 5 begins with the healing at the pool of Bethesda on the Sabbath, which provokes a dispute. Jesus claims to do what the Father does (5:17–19), to give life and to judge (5:21–22). Then: ‘Amen, amen, I say unto you, that he who heareth my word, and believeth him that sent me, hath life everlasting; and cometh not into judgment, but is passed from death to life’ (5:24). He continues: ‘the hour cometh, wherein all that are in the graves shall hear the voice of the Son of God. And they that have done good things, shall come forth unto the resurrection of life; but they that have done evil, unto the resurrection of judgment’ (5:28–29).",
 who="Eternal-security preaching uses this verse as a three-fold guarantee: the believer has eternal life (present possession), will not come into judgment (future), and has passed from death to life (completed). ‘If you could lose it, it wasn’t eternal.’",
 lang="“Heareth” (akouōn) and “believeth” (pisteuōn) are present participles: the one hearing, the one believing. “Hath” (echei) is present. “Is passed” (metabebēken) is perfect: has crossed over and remains.\n\n“Judgment” (krisis) in John often means condemnation (3:18–19).",
 steps=[
  ["Present possession is not irreversible possession","Eternal life is called ‘eternal’ because of its source and quality (the life of God), not because the human recipient cannot lose it. Adam had the life of grace and lost it. John himself: ‘no murderer hath eternal life abiding in himself’ (1 John 3:15). A Christian who commits murder does not then have eternal life abiding in him."],
  ["The participles describe a continuing state","The promise attaches to ‘the one who keeps hearing and believing’. If a person stops believing, he no longer fits the description. Jesus: ‘If you continue in my word, you shall be my disciples indeed’ (8:31)."],
  ["The same discourse judges by works","5:29: the resurrection of life for those who have done good, of judgment for those who have done evil. Jesus sees no contradiction between ‘believers do not come into condemnation’ and ‘people will be judged by what they have done’, because living faith does good."],
  ["John 15 shows branches can be cut off","‘Every branch in me, that beareth not fruit, he will take away’ (15:2); ‘If any one abide not in me, he shall be cast forth as a branch, and shall wither, and they shall gather him up, and cast him into the fire, and he burneth’ (15:6). These are branches ‘in me’, real disciples."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, Tractates on John 22 (c. 414)","The passage from death to life is the first resurrection, of the soul by faith; the second, of the body, follows at the end; those who rise now in soul must persevere to rise to life in the body (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on John, ch. 5, lect. 4","‘Hath life everlasting’ in hope and in its beginning, through grace; the believer ‘cometh not into judgment’ of condemnation if he perseveres (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“If it can be lost, it isn’t eternal.”","Then Adam’s life of grace was not grace, and the angels who fell never had God’s favour. ‘Eternal’ describes the kind of life (God’s), not a guarantee against our refusing it."]],
 talk="“Jesus says the one who keeps hearing and believing has eternal life right now. That’s grace, and it’s wonderful. But a few verses later he says people will rise to life or to judgment depending on whether they’ve done good or evil. And in John 15 he says branches in him that stop bearing fruit are cut off. So eternal life is a present gift we have to abide in, not a ticket we can’t lose.”",
 avoid="Do not deny present possession of eternal life; Catholics who are in grace have it.",
 read=["CCC 1996–1997, 1861","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on John, ch. 5","Augustine, Tractates on John 19–23"])

add('john',10,28,'John 10:28','"No man shall pluck them out of my hand"',
 'This is the classic text for "once saved, always saved": a true believer is held by Christ and the Father and can never be lost.',
 ['The promise is about outside powers: no one can snatch Christ\'s sheep from him. It does not say a sheep cannot walk away.',
  'The sheep are those who "hear my voice… and follow me" (10:27), present tense: they keep on following.',
  'Elsewhere Scripture warns real disciples that they can fall: "he that thinketh himself to stand, let him take heed lest he fall" (1 Corinthians 10:12); John 15:6; Hebrews 6:4–6.',
  'Catholics take great comfort in this verse: no devil, persecution or power can take us from Christ. Only our own free and grave sin can separate us.'],
 'Christ\'s hold on his sheep is stronger than any enemy; the only danger is our own refusal to follow.',
 'CCC 1861, 2016; Trent, Session 6, ch. 13, canon 23',
 tags=('assurance',))
D('john',10,28,
 ctx="John 10 is the Good Shepherd discourse. Jesus is the door of the sheep (10:7) and the good shepherd who lays down his life (10:11). At the feast of Dedication, the Jews ask him to say plainly if he is the Christ (10:24). He answers: ‘you do not believe, because you are not of my sheep. My sheep hear my voice: and I know them, and they follow me. And I give them life everlasting; and they shall not perish for ever, and no man shall pluck them out of my hand. That which my Father hath given me, is greater than all: and no one can snatch them out of the hand of my Father. I and the Father are one’ (10:26–30).",
 who="Calvinist and Baptist theology uses this verse as the strongest proof of the ‘perseverance of the saints’: the elect, held in the hands of both Son and Father, cannot be lost. If a professed believer falls away, he was never truly one of the sheep.",
 lang="“They shall not perish for ever” (ou mē apolōntai eis ton aiōna): emphatic double negative, ‘they will certainly not perish’. “Pluck/snatch” (harpasei, harpazein) means to seize by force, as a wolf does (10:12, the same verb: ‘the wolf catcheth (harpazei) and scattereth the sheep’).\n\n“Hear… follow” (akouousin… akolouthousin) are present tense: they keep hearing and following.",
 steps=[
  ["The threat in view is outside force","The verb harpazō is the wolf’s verb (10:12). Jesus promises that no thief, wolf or hireling, no human or demonic power, can tear his sheep from him. Catholics wholeheartedly affirm this: Satan cannot take a soul from Christ by force."],
  ["The promise is to those who keep following","10:27 defines the sheep: they hear and follow (present tense). The promise of 10:28 is for those who do this. Jesus does not say a sheep cannot stop following. Elsewhere he speaks of disciples who ‘went back; and walked no more with him’ (6:67) and of Judas, whom the Father had given him and who was lost (17:12)."],
  ["Scripture warns believers that they can fall away","Paul: ‘thou standest by faith… otherwise thou also shalt be cut off’ (Romans 11:20–22); ‘you are fallen from grace’ (Galatians 5:4). Peter: ‘if, flying from the pollutions of the world… they be again entangled in them and overcome: their latter state is become unto them worse than the former’ (2 Peter 2:20). Jesus: branches in him cast into the fire (John 15:6). If falling away were impossible, these warnings would be pointless."],
  ["The Catholic reading keeps both comfort and warning","Final perseverance is a gift of God (Trent, Session 6, ch. 13), and God is faithful. We should have firm hope that he who began a good work will complete it (Philippians 1:6). But we can resist grace, and so we pray daily, ‘lead us not into temptation’. Those who persevere to the end were held by Christ; that is the elect. But no one can know with the certainty of faith that he is among them (canon 16)."],
  ["The ‘never truly saved’ answer undermines assurance","If every apostate was never a real believer, then no one can know he is a real believer until he dies faithful: someone who seems to have every mark of grace may be a false convert. So eternal security does not actually provide the certainty it promises."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, Tractates on John 48.6 (c. 416)","The wolf cannot snatch, the thief cannot steal, the robber cannot kill those whom Christ knows as his own, the predestined; but this is known to God (paraphrased)."],
  ["Augustine, On Rebuke and Grace 13.40","Of those who are called and justified, some do not persevere; we do not know who will, so we must not be proud (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on John, ch. 10, lect. 5","No one can snatch the sheep by violence; but a sheep can depart of its own will through sin (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“If no one can snatch them, that includes the sheep themselves.”","The sheep don’t ‘snatch’ themselves; snatching is forcible seizure by another. The question of the sheep’s own will is answered in 10:27: they follow. Judas, ‘given’ to Christ (17:12), was lost by his own choice."],
  ["“Those who fall away show they were never sheep (1 John 2:19).”","See the note on 1 John 5:13. That verse describes particular schismatics. And it cannot explain Hebrews 6:4–6 or 2 Peter 2:20–22, which describe true conversion followed by falling."]],
 talk="“John 10:28 is one of my favourite verses too. No one can rip a Christian out of Christ’s hand: not the devil, not persecution, nothing. But look at verse 27: his sheep are those who keep listening and keep following. The danger isn’t a wolf snatching us; it’s us walking away, like Judas did. So we trust Christ completely, and we keep following him.”",
 avoid="Do not tell Calvinists they don’t care about holiness, or that they think they can sin freely; they teach that true believers will persevere in holiness.",
 read=["Council of Trent, Session 6, chs. 12–13, canons 15–16, 22–23","CCC 1821, 2016","Augustine, On Rebuke and Grace; On the Gift of Perseverance","I. Howard Marshall (1969) Kept by the Power of God. London: Epworth (a Protestant scholar against unconditional security)"])

add('romans',8,38,'Romans 8:38–39','"Nothing shall be able to separate us from the love of God"',
 'Paul\'s list (death, life, angels, powers, things present or to come, any creature) is read as a guarantee that a believer can never be separated from God, so salvation cannot be lost.',
 ['Every item on the list is an outside power or circumstance. Sin, our own free choice to turn from God, is not on the list.',
  'God\'s love for us never ends; the question is whether we remain in it. Jesus: "Abide in my love… If you keep my commandments, you shall abide in my love" (John 15:9–10).',
  'Three chapters later, Paul warns believers: "otherwise thou also shalt be cut off" (Romans 11:22).',
  'Catholics read the verse as the great charter of Christian hope: nothing outside us can defeat God\'s love.'],
 'No power in creation can tear us from God\'s love. Only our own grave sin can turn us away from it, and even then God\'s mercy calls us back.',
 'CCC 1821, 1861; Romans 11:20–22',
 tags=('assurance',))
D('romans',8,38,
 ctx="Romans 8 is the climax of Paul’s teaching on life in the Spirit. ‘If God be for us, who is against us?’ (8:31). ‘Who shall separate us from the love of Christ? shall tribulation? or distress? or famine? or nakedness? or danger? or persecution? or the sword?’ (8:35). ‘For I am sure that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor powers, nor things present, nor things to come, nor might, nor height, nor depth, nor any other creature, shall be able to separate us from the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord’ (8:38–39). But earlier in the chapter: ‘if you live according to the flesh, you shall die’ (8:13); ‘heirs… yet so, if we suffer with him’ (8:17).",
 who="This passage is used as the final word for eternal security: ‘nor any other creature’ includes you; therefore not even your own sin can separate you from God.",
 lang="“I am sure” (pepeismai, perfect): I am fully persuaded. “Any other creature” (tis ktisis hetera): ‘any other created thing’.",
 steps=[
  ["The list is of external powers","Tribulation, persecution, sword (8:35), death and life, angels and principalities, present and future, height and depth: all are forces that act on the Christian from outside. Paul is answering the fear that suffering or spiritual powers could cut us off from God. They cannot."],
  ["‘Any other creature’ means anything else in creation of that kind","Paul’s point is that no power in the created order can overcome God’s love. Reading ‘any creature’ to include our own free refusal makes Paul contradict 8:13 (‘if you live according to the flesh, you shall die’) and 11:22 (‘otherwise thou also shalt be cut off’)."],
  ["God’s love does not stop; we can stop receiving it","God loves even sinners in hell, in the sense that he wills their existence; but they have refused communion with him. Jesus distinguishes God’s love toward us and our abiding in it: ‘abide in my love. If you keep my commandments, you shall abide in my love’ (John 15:9–10). Jude 21: ‘Keep yourselves in the love of God.’ You cannot be told to keep yourself in something you cannot leave."],
  ["The condition stated in 8:17","‘Heirs indeed of God, and joint heirs with Christ: yet so, if we suffer with him, that we may be also glorified with him.’ The ‘if’ is part of the same chapter."]],
 fathers=[
  ["John Chrysostom, Homilies on Romans 15 (c. 390)","Paul is speaking of his own love for Christ that no tribulation could overcome, and of God’s love that is stronger than every power (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Romans, ch. 8, lect. 7","Nothing external can separate the saints from charity, though the free will can turn away through sin; Paul speaks of the predestined, who in fact will not be separated (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“You are a creature, so you can’t separate yourself either.”","Then Paul contradicts himself three chapters later (11:22) and five verses earlier (8:13). Jude 21 tells Christians to keep themselves in God’s love, which presupposes they could fail to."]],
 talk="“Romans 8 is the most hopeful chapter in the Bible: nothing in all creation can tear us from God’s love. But notice everything on the list is something outside us: death, angels, persecution, the future. What isn’t on the list is our own sin. That’s why Paul also says in the same chapter, ‘if you live according to the flesh, you will die’, and Jude says ‘keep yourselves in the love of God’.”",
 avoid="Do not reduce this verse to a warning; it is first a song of confidence. Lead with the comfort.",
 read=["CCC 1817–1821","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Romans, ch. 8, lect. 7","Council of Trent, Session 6, ch. 13"])

add('isaiah',64,6,'Isaiah 64:6','"All our justices as the rag of a menstruous woman"',
 'Often quoted as "all our righteousnesses are as filthy rags" to prove that even the good works of Christians are worthless and sinful before God, so they can play no part in salvation and merit is impossible.',
 ['In context, Isaiah is confessing the sins of unfaithful Israel during exile: "behold thou art angry, and we have sinned" (64:5). He describes the people\'s works while they were turned away from God.',
  'Isaiah himself praises true righteousness: the fast God chooses, feeding the hungry and sheltering the poor, brings light and healing (58:6–8).',
  'The New Testament says the opposite of the works of the saints: "the fine linen are the justifications of saints" (Revelation 19:8), and God "is not unjust, that he should forget your work" (Hebrews 6:10).',
  'Works done in grace, by the Holy Spirit, are God\'s work in us (Philippians 2:13), and God does not call his own work filthy.'],
 'Works done apart from God cannot save. Works done in grace are pleasing to God and rewarded by him.',
 'Trent, Session 6, ch. 16, canons 25–26, 32; CCC 2006–2011',
 tags=('salvation','grace'))
D('isaiah',64,6,
 ctx="Isaiah 63:7–64:12 is a communal lament, a prayer of Israel in exile. It recalls God’s mercies in the Exodus (63:7–14), then pleads: ‘Look down from heaven… where is thy zeal?’ (63:15). ‘Behold thou art angry, and we have sinned: in them we have been always, and we shall be saved. And we are all become as one unclean, and all our justices as the rag of a menstruous woman: and we have all fallen as a leaf, and our iniquities, like the wind, have taken us away’ (64:5–6). ‘There is none that calleth upon thy name’ (64:7). The prayer ends begging God not to be angry for ever (64:9).",
 who="Reformed theology uses this verse to show total depravity: even the best works of fallen humans, including Christians, are tainted by sin, so they cannot merit anything before God. Luther and Calvin taught that every good work of the justified is still sinful in God’s sight if judged by strict justice (Luther, Against Latomus, 1521; Calvin, Institutes III.14.9–11). Therefore justification must rest entirely on Christ’s imputed righteousness, never on works.",
 lang="Hebrew: ukhebeged ‘iddim kol-tsidqotenu: ‘and like a garment of menstruation all our righteous deeds’. ‘Iddim refers to menstrual cloths, ritually unclean under Leviticus 15:19–24. The image is of ritual uncleanness that excludes from worship, matching ‘we are all become as one unclean’ (tame’).\n\nVerse numbering: 64:6 in the Douay, Vulgate and KJV; 64:5 in the Hebrew Bible.",
 steps=[
  ["It is the confession of an unfaithful people","Read 64:5–7. The people have sinned ‘always’; ‘there is none that calleth upon thy name’. The ‘righteous deeds’ in question are the religious acts of Israel while it was in rebellion: sacrifices and fasts performed without conversion of heart. Isaiah condemned exactly those in chapter 1: ‘bring no more sacrifice in vain… your new moons… my soul hateth’ (1:13–14)."],
  ["Isaiah praises the righteousness God gives","‘Is not this rather the fast that I have chosen? loose the bands of wickedness… Deal thy bread to the hungry… Then shall thy light break forth as the morning… and thy justice shall go before thy face’ (58:6–8). ‘Keep ye judgment, and do justice: for my salvation is near to come’ (56:1). Isaiah does not think all righteousness is filth; he distinguishes hypocritical religion from true justice."],
  ["Works done in grace are God’s own work","‘We are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus in good works, which God hath prepared that we should walk in them’ (Ephesians 2:10). ‘It is God who worketh in you, both to will and to accomplish’ (Philippians 2:13). If the good works of the justified are filthy rags, then the Holy Spirit produces filth. Scripture instead says: ‘the fine linen are the justifications (dikaiōmata, righteous deeds) of saints’ (Revelation 19:8)."],
  ["Scripture speaks constantly of God rewarding good works","‘God is not unjust, that he should forget your work, and the love which you have shewn in his name’ (Hebrews 6:10). ‘Whosoever shall give to drink… a cup of cold water… he shall not lose his reward’ (Matthew 10:42). ‘Come, ye blessed… for I was hungry, and you gave me to eat’ (Matthew 25:34–35). ‘There is laid up for me a crown of justice, which the Lord the just judge will render to me’ (2 Timothy 4:8)."],
  ["Trent’s balanced teaching","Trent condemns the claims that the just sin in every good work (canon 25) and that the good works of the justified do not merit eternal life (canon 32). It also says Christians must not trust in themselves or glory in themselves but in the Lord, ‘whose bounty towards all men is so great, that he will have the things which are his own gifts be their merits’ (Session 6, ch. 16)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Jerome, Commentary on Isaiah, book 17 (c. 410)","Reads the verse as the confession of the Jewish people under God’s anger, recognizing that their works done in unfaithfulness were unclean (paraphrased)."],
  ["Augustine, Letter 194, 5.19","God crowns his own gifts when he rewards our merits."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 6, ch. 16 and canons 25, 32 (1547)","Good works of the justified truly merit increase of grace and eternal life, as gifts of God."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Even a Christian’s best work is mixed with selfishness.”","Often true, and Catholics confess it daily. But mixed motives make a work imperfect, not sinful through and through. And God, who knows our weakness, accepts the good he has worked in us for Christ’s sake (1 Peter 2:5: ‘spiritual sacrifices, acceptable to God by Jesus Christ’)."],
  ["“Isaiah says ‘all our righteousnesses’, including his own.”","He speaks in solidarity with his people, as Daniel does (Daniel 9:5–7) and Nehemiah does (Nehemiah 1:6). This is liturgical confession, not a philosophical claim about every act of every believer."]],
 talk="“Isaiah is praying for Israel during exile, confessing that the people had been worshipping while living in sin, and their religious acts were unclean. He’s right: works done without God are worthless. But Isaiah also says fasting by feeding the hungry makes God’s light break forth (58:8). And the New Testament says the saints are clothed in fine linen, which is their righteous deeds (Revelation 19:8). Works done in God’s grace aren’t filthy rags; they’re what the Holy Spirit does in us.”",
 avoid="Do not say that our natural goodness, apart from grace, can earn salvation; that is Pelagianism and the Church condemned it (Council of Carthage, 418).",
 read=["Council of Trent, Session 6, ch. 16, canons 25–26, 32","CCC 2006–2011","ST I-II q.114","Jerome, Commentary on Isaiah, on 64:6"])
